{
  "version": 1,
  "about": "The drawn jog display's messages (#455): each state and the exact bytes on the WD port. The Swift encoder (Kit tests) and the C decoder (firmware tests) both walk this file. Host to unit: F0 00 02 0B 7F 10 <cmd> <len hi> <len lo> <op> <args> F7, len counting op and args (14 bits). Unit to host: the reply header F0 00 02 0B 01 10 instead. cmd 0x20: Denon's display firmware (wd 00.50) drops it silently, with no reply (#466). Ops: 00 capability, 01 begin, 02 end, 03 deck, 04 text, 05 tempo, 06 time, 07 state, 08 beat, 09 needle, 0A meter, 0B phase; no op is reserved now. Meter is the deck in front's mixer channel: its level, then the peak being held, each 0-127 (#461); 0 and 0 with no mixer feedback. Text (#458) is 04 <field> <chars>, framed by len (no length of its own): field 0 title, 1 artist, 2 key, 3 untitled words (with an empty title, the big words the deck shows instead: \"No track\", \"OSC clip\", or the player type's own name, as JogDisplayWords; empty while there is a title). The chip draws them with its own caption, \"Load a track\" for an empty deck or a track and \"no title\" for the rest. Empty text (len 2) clears its field. Chars are Windows-1252 codes: below 0x80 as themselves, 0x80-0xFF as 7F then the code minus 0x80. Every defined Windows-1252 code is text; 0x81 is the box, which the host sends for a character it can't map; the other undefined codes (8D, 8F, 90, 9D), codes below 0x20, a 7F as the last byte and a field over 3 make the message malformed. The cap: at most 96 bytes of chars after escapes (len at most 98, the whole message at most 108 bytes). The host cuts plain text to the cap at a character boundary, never inside an escape, and adds no ellipsis; the chip adds the ellipsis where a line doesn't fit. Text entries carry the host's input (text) and the codes the chip decodes (codes). Tempo (#458) is 05 hi lo, tenths of a BPM as 14 bits, high then low 7 bits; 0 is none (drawn as a dash). The host rounds once to tenths, sends at most 16383 and sends 0 for no tempo. Time (#460) is 06 eh el lh ll: the deck's elapsed time, then its track's length, in whole seconds, each 14 bits, high then low 7 bits; the host clamps each to 16383. Length 0 is unknown: the chip draws no time and no progress ring. The chip works out what's left. The host sends it only when the shown second or the length changes (at most 4 a second). Phase (#462) is 0B hi lo, how far the deck in front is from the master clock's beat (beat phase, not bar phase: a deck whole beats off still reads in phase), as a 14-bit two's complement count of 1/16384 beats, high then low 7 bits: -8191 to 8191, negative behind. -8192 (40 00) is unknown: the deck isn't playing, there's no master clock, or no offset has come since the deck loaded; the chip draws the plain rule. 0 is in phase: the rule with a center tick. Anything else draws a bar from the center, left for behind and right for ahead. The host sends round(offset x 16384) clamped to 8191, with its own hysteresis (0 while in phase) and wrap guard. The chip starts each deck at unknown. Beat is one byte, the master clock's beat 1-4, or 0 with no master clock (no section lit). Needle is the deck's place in its bar as a 14-bit angle, high then low 7 bits, 0-16383 for one turn clockwise from 12 o'clock (the scale of the unit's own pitch-bend needle). State is one flags byte: bit 0 loaded, 1 playing, 2 play pending, 3 cue held, 4 loop on, 5 feedback stale; bit 6 is reserved and makes the message malformed. Colors go as two 7-bit bytes per channel, high then low. Player: 0 empty, 1 track, 2 OSC clip, 3 MIDI clip, 4 sequencer, 5 other. Each op but text has one exact length (capability 2, begin 2, end 2, deck 9, tempo 3, time 5, state 2, beat 2, needle 3, meter 3, phase 3, counting the op); any other is ignored, as is version 0. Entries with \"direction\": \"unit\" go from the unit to the host; the rest from the host. Sequence rules (#464): after begin the host sends the full state, the deck then the state; every deck message is followed by its state, and a deck message clears flags 0-4 until it arrives. After the state the host sends the four texts, the tempo and the phase, then the beat, the needle, the time and the meter; a deck message that changes the deck clears the texts, the tempo, the phase (to unknown) and the time on the chip, and the host sends the new deck's time right after. The phase also goes on change, at most 30 a second. While drawing, the host sends begin, the deck, the state, the texts, the tempo, the phase and the time again once a second (the heartbeat); an unchanged one changes nothing on the chip; image mode sends none. On the chip: begin while drawing is a no-op; begin with no frame (after a chip reset) allocates it once, as the capability question does, and if that fails the chip stays in stock mode and draws nothing; no drawn-display message for 3 s draws the stale look, and any message clears it; only end or a reboot leaves drawn mode. The host sets loaded, playing, play pending (#459) and stale; cue held (the deck's CUE button down) and loop on (Syndicate's Loop) are the host's too (#463), sent on change; the chip draws neither (the user's choice), but loop on holds the time's warning (#524). The chip shows play state through the needle, as the unit does today: it moves while playing and holds while paused (#459). The one other thing playing changes is the time's last-minute warning (#460, #524): while playing and not looping, with a known length, the time and the ring's fill are steady red (0xFF3B30) from 60 s left, and from 30 s left blink at 1 Hz, red for the first 500 ms after each change of the time, then white (0xEDEDEA), for every deck color; paused or looping, the normal colors, and a state that clears the loop brings the warning back at once. In drawn mode the host sends no pitch-bend needle (E0) and no play light (note 1); on end or a fallback to images it sends both again, current, just before.",
